-- title:
--   Left secant of the Gauss order equals the top dominant index
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a complete discrete valuation domain (a domain, a discrete valuation ring, adically complete for its maximal ideal), let $\varpi \in W$ be irreducible, and let $e, q \ge 1$ be natural numbers. Write $v := \varpi \mapsto q\cdot \mathrm{addVal}_W$, i.e. the function $w \mapsto q\cdot v_W(w) \in \mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$ obtained by scaling the normalised additive valuation of $W$ by $q$. Let $x$ be a nonzero element of the crossing model $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\;W\;(\varpi^{e})$, the quotient of $W[[X_0,X_1]]$ (two-variable multivariate power series) by the ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - \varpi^{e}$, and let $ab = (a,b)$ be a pair of one-variable power series with $b$ of vanishing constant coefficient such that the class of $\mathrm{inU}\,a + \mathrm{inV}\,b$ is $x$, where $\mathrm{inU}\,a$ is the bivariate series supported on multidegrees with second entry $0$ whose coefficients are those of $a$, and $\mathrm{inV}\,b$ is the analogue in the second variable. Let $p$ satisfy $1 \le p \le qe$, and assume that $\sup \mathrm{dominantIndices}\,v\,(qe)\,p\,ab$ belongs to $\mathrm{dominantIndices}\,v\,(qe)\,(p-1)\,ab$, where $\mathrm{dominantIndices}\,v\,E\,t\,ab$ is the set of $n \in \mathbb{Z}$ with $v(\mathrm{nfCoeff}\,ab\,n) + \mathrm{annulusWeight}\,E\,t\,(\mathrm{nfExponent}\,n)$ equal to $\mathrm{repGaussOrder}\,v\,E\,t\,(\mathrm{inU}\,a + \mathrm{inV}\,b) = \inf_{d} \bigl(v(\mathrm{coeff}_d) + \mathrm{annulusWeight}\,E\,t\,d\bigr)$. Then, with $G(t) := \mathrm{gaussOrder}\,v\,(\varpi^{e})\,(qe)\,t\,x = \sup\{\mathrm{repGaussOrder}\,v\,(qe)\,t\,F : F \mapsto x\}$, the integer difference $G(p).\mathrm{toNat} - G(p-1).\mathrm{toNat}$ equals $\sup \mathrm{dominantIndices}\,v\,(qe)\,p\,ab$.
--
--   This is one step of the interior slope law for the Gauss-order profile $t \mapsto G(t)$ on the annulus attached to a width-$e$ crossing model: under the stated no-kink condition at $p$, the secant slope of the profile over $[p-1,p]$ is read off as the largest dominant index at depth $p$. It is used by the lemmas that identify the dominant index sets along the annulus and that compute the circle index drop.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_gaussOrder_sub_pred_eq_sSup_dominantIndices.lean

import Mathlib
